00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:55,640
and some other things.

263
00:10:55,640 --> 00:10:57,000
So he was the guy.

264
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:57,720
You know this guy?

265
00:10:57,720 --> 00:10:58,200
Yeah.

266
00:10:58,200 --> 00:11:01,120
So was it on the national news when he died?

267
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:03,880
Was it national news in China when he died?

268
00:11:03,880 --> 00:11:05,560
He also, when he was over 100, he

269
00:11:05,560 --> 00:11:09,040
started writing books attacking the government, which is,

270
00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:10,920
we mean, he does seem like he was thinking,

271
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:13,400
oh, I don't think you're going to punish me now.

272
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:15,120
103.

273
00:11:15,120 --> 00:11:17,960
I think you'll just wait me out.

274
00:11:17,960 --> 00:11:19,160
Anyway.

275
00:11:19,160 --> 00:11:20,080
OK.

276
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:25,240
So let's go and, I guess, let's see.

277
00:11:25,240 --> 00:11:27,560
So now let's go and start with a finite repeated game

278
00:11:27,560 --> 00:11:29,200
to see how they work.

279
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:32,280
So let's go and use the payoffs from last week.

280
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:34,680
And suppose there's two players who do the following.

281
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:37,080
First, they go and play Prisoner's Lemma.

282
00:11:37,080 --> 00:11:39,720
And secondly, they then immediately

283
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:43,840
do a coordination game using the payoffs we see.

284
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:45,120
So this is the repeat.

285
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:47,120
So there's only, there's just two games.

286
00:11:47,120 --> 00:11:47,640
That's it.

287
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:48,320
And then it ends.

288
00:11:48,320 --> 00:11:49,200
Everybody knows this.

289
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:50,520
It's a finite repetition.

290
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:53,320
Starts with Prisoner's Lemma, then concludes

291
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:56,080
with a coordination game.

292
00:11:56,080 --> 00:11:59,280
So one possibility, which is always true when you're

293
00:11:59,280 --> 00:12:01,880
doing repeated games, is there is an equilibrium where you

294
00:12:01,880 --> 00:12:04,680
just repeat whatever the equilibria were

295
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:06,440
for the one-shot games.

296
00:12:06,440 --> 00:12:09,280
So you could have an equilibrium where people just

297
00:12:09,280 --> 00:12:11,000
don't cooperate in the first round,

298
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,200
and then they both play left, or they both play right

299
00:12:13,200 --> 00:12:14,680
in the second round, the end.

300
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:16,400
So that's totally fine.

301
00:12:16,400 --> 00:12:20,160
But there's actually a perennial superior equilibrium,

302
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:22,240
which is now possible.

303
00:12:22,240 --> 00:12:24,640
And this is going to sound like one of the weirdest things

304
00:12:24,640 --> 00:12:26,360
you're ever going to hear in game theory.

305
00:12:26,360 --> 00:12:29,840
But I swear to you, it's actually fine.

306
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:32,280
So here's what I'm going to propose.

307
00:12:32,280 --> 00:12:34,280
And again, when I propose it, you're

308
00:12:34,280 --> 00:12:36,040
thinking like you're cheating somehow.

309
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:38,840
You're doing something really weird here.

310
00:12:38,840 --> 00:12:40,520
But yeah, well, it's weird.

311
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:41,760
It's creative.

312
00:12:41,760 --> 00:12:42,760
It's a creative thing.

313
00:12:42,760 --> 00:12:43,560
I didn't create it.

314
00:12:43,560 --> 00:12:45,100
Or someone else explained this to me,

315
00:12:45,100 --> 00:12:46,960
and I'm just repeating it to you.

316
00:12:46,960 --> 00:12:51,640
But here is a creative way of dealing with a solution.

317
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:56,560
So suppose, we still have these payoffs out.

318
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:58,880
Suppose that what we do is we say, look,

319
00:12:58,880 --> 00:13:02,280
if either of us fail to cooperate in turn one,

320
00:13:02,280 --> 00:13:04,720
then we both play left.

321
00:13:04,720 --> 00:13:07,000
As long as both of us cooperate in turn one,

322
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:08,000
then we both play right.

323
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:15,600
All right, so and now, once this occurs, what's going to happen?

324
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:18,400
So if both sides believe this is going to happen,

325
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:21,600
then both sides actually will want to cooperate in turn one

326
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:24,080
and will then play right in turn two.

327
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:25,400
All right, well, wait a second.

328
00:13:25,400 --> 00:13:26,860
The first turn's a prisoner's law.

329
00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:29,240
So just in terms, so never mind experiments,

330
00:13:29,240 --> 00:13:31,920
but in terms of pure theory, how could that occur?

331
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:36,040
So if we go back to the payoffs, so let's see.

332
00:13:36,040 --> 00:13:39,120
I think it was, I think the original payoffs were for,

333
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:46,440
let's see, cooperate, don't, and cooperate, don't.

334
00:13:46,440 --> 00:13:47,800
And then it was this one, right?

335
00:13:47,800 --> 00:13:48,640
It was 5-5.

336
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:50,600
Was it 0-0 for don't, don't?

337
00:13:50,600 --> 00:13:51,480
That's the new thing.

338
00:13:51,480 --> 00:13:52,040
That's right.

339
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:53,560
Yeah, 0-0.

340
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:56,760
And then if you cooperate and, let's see.

341
00:13:56,760 --> 00:13:58,720
Or was it, or then it was 1-1, right?

342
00:13:58,720 --> 00:13:59,320
Wasn't it 1-1?

343
00:13:59,320 --> 00:14:00,120
Yeah, it was 1-1.

344
00:14:00,120 --> 00:14:02,800
OK, so that's 1-1.

345
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:04,880
And then if you cooperate, the other guy doesn't.

346
00:14:04,880 --> 00:14:06,080
So if you get 0, you get 6.

347
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:08,520
And if it's the other way around, you get 6, you get 0.

348
00:14:08,520 --> 00:14:14,520
OK, so now, we can just repeat this creative way

349
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:17,440
of getting out, of getting to a better equilibrium.

350
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:21,200
So it says, look, as long as we both play cooperate in turn 1,

351
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:23,480
then we both play right in turn 2.

352
00:14:23,480 --> 00:14:25,320
If, on the other hand, either of us

353
00:14:25,320 --> 00:14:28,600
fail to play cooperate in turn 1,

354
00:14:28,600 --> 00:14:31,520
then we both play left in turn 2.

355
00:14:31,520 --> 00:14:36,840
Now, why would you not want to deviate from this?

356
00:14:36,840 --> 00:14:40,080
Well, so as long as you stick to the equilibrium,

357
00:14:40,080 --> 00:14:40,740
what do you get?

358
00:14:40,740 --> 00:14:42,240
You get 5 in turn 1.

359
00:14:42,240 --> 00:14:44,520
And you get 5 in turn 2, so you get 10, right?

360
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:46,280
So you get 5 plus 5.

361
00:14:46,280 --> 00:14:48,480
All right, now you maybe tend to say, ah, well,

362
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:51,400
I'll go betray you in turn 1, and I'll get 6 myself.

363
00:14:51,400 --> 00:14:52,640
So that's going to work out.

364
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:56,520
But then, based upon this equilibrium, you're over here.

365
00:14:56,520 --> 00:14:59,200
And now, since one of you actually played

366
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:01,440
don't cooperate in turn 1, what should you

367
00:15:01,440 --> 00:15:04,440
expect your opponent will do?

368
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:05,560
Left.

369
00:15:05,560 --> 00:15:09,080
And if your opponent plays left, what is the least bad thing

370
00:15:09,080 --> 00:15:10,960
for you to do?

371
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:11,960
Play left.

372
00:15:11,960 --> 00:15:13,720
So then you get 3.

373
00:15:13,720 --> 00:15:19,040
And remind me, which is bigger, 5 plus 5 or 6 plus 3?

374
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:20,120
5 plus 5.

375
00:15:20,120 --> 00:15:22,360
Yes, so yes, that's actually better.

376
00:15:22,360 --> 00:15:24,840
Now, almost everyone wants to, no, no, no, no, no,

377
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:26,480
you're not going to trade me with this.

378
00:15:26,480 --> 00:15:28,600
No, I'm going to not cooperate in turn 1,

379
00:15:28,600 --> 00:15:31,160
and then I'm going to play right in turn 2.

380
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:32,840
Well, what happens if you do that?

381
00:15:32,840 --> 00:15:34,760
Then you get 6 in turn 1.

382
00:15:34,760 --> 00:15:36,960
And then, again, holding constant the assumption

383
00:15:36,960 --> 00:15:40,160
your opponent plays the equilibrium, he'll play left.

384
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:42,320
And if you play right when your opponent plays left,

385
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:43,040
what do you get?

386
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:43,560
0.

387
00:15:43,560 --> 00:15:44,960
0.

388
00:15:44,960 --> 00:15:47,920
All right, and remind me, wait, is 6 plus 0,

389
00:15:47,920 --> 00:15:50,680
is that greater or less than 10?

390
00:15:50,680 --> 00:15:53,760
Yes, it's even worse.

391
00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:56,600
Now, it's like there's some kind of trickery going on here.

392
00:15:56,600 --> 00:15:58,320
Like, how can you do this?

393
00:15:58,320 --> 00:15:59,680
How can you pull this off?

394
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:03,960
And the answer is that there are two equilibria in turn 2.

395
00:16:03,960 --> 00:16:10,640
So if play in turn 2 depends upon turn 1,

396
00:16:10,640 --> 00:16:13,600
then your opponent can actually punish you

397
00:16:13,600 --> 00:16:17,040
in a way that is consistent with their own interests.

398
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:20,320
Because if you both assume that you're both going to play,

399
00:16:20,320 --> 00:16:22,880
well, what's expected?

400
00:16:22,880 --> 00:16:27,160
And then one person doesn't, the result for him is even worse.

401
00:16:27,160 --> 00:16:28,880
So basically, you can make your opponent

402
00:16:28,880 --> 00:16:31,360
accomplish to his own punishment by putting him

403
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:33,720
in a situation where it's like either take your punishment

404
00:16:33,720 --> 00:16:35,960
or you'll get an even worse punishment.

405
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:37,480
Like, well, in that case, I guess

406
00:16:37,480 --> 00:16:38,360
I'll just take my punishment.

407
00:16:38,360 --> 00:16:39,280
Wait a second.

408
00:16:39,280 --> 00:16:41,800
If I know I'm going to have to take my punishment in turn 2,

409
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:43,960
I don't want to break the agreement in turn 1

410
00:16:43,960 --> 00:16:45,320
or break the agreement in turn 2.

411
00:16:45,320 --> 00:16:47,040
It doesn't even have to be an agreement.

412
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:49,760
It can just be an informal understanding.

413
00:16:49,760 --> 00:16:52,160
If you might have never met the person,

414
00:16:52,160 --> 00:16:54,760
never communicated with them, but if, say, there's

415
00:16:54,760 --> 00:16:58,240
just a cultural understanding that the proper response

416
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:01,840
to non-cooperation in turn 1 is left in turn 2,

417
00:17:01,840 --> 00:17:04,120
then everyone will want to go and cooperate in turn 1

418
00:17:04,120 --> 00:17:05,520
and play right in turn 2.

419
00:17:05,520 --> 00:17:06,320
Yes.

420
00:17:06,320 --> 00:17:09,960
But you could still, if you were on turn 2

421
00:17:09,960 --> 00:17:12,320
and you had not cooperated and gotten to 6

422
00:17:12,320 --> 00:17:14,200
and you were just going to still play right,

423
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:17,800
your opponent is still in his best interest to also play right.

424
00:17:17,800 --> 00:17:19,600
But remember, you're not doing Nash equilibrium right now,

425
00:17:19,600 --> 00:17:20,520
are you?

426
00:17:20,520 --> 00:17:21,040
No.

427
00:17:21,040 --> 00:17:21,540
Yes.

428
00:17:21,540 --> 00:17:25,760
And that is the key here to why we can get more Nash

429
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:26,760
equilibrium.

430
00:17:26,760 --> 00:17:29,520
Now, again, you may say that the Nash equilibrium concept

431
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:31,080
is strange.

432
00:17:31,080 --> 00:17:33,840
And there are some strange things about it.

433
00:17:33,840 --> 00:17:36,040
Just in terms of the logic of the Nash equilibrium

434
00:17:36,040 --> 00:17:39,200
where you assume that everyone else does what sticks

435
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:41,920
to the equilibrium by yourself, the idea

436
00:17:41,920 --> 00:17:44,040
is there's only one person's behavior you control,

437
00:17:44,040 --> 00:17:45,200
which is your own.

438
00:17:45,200 --> 00:17:48,600
Everybody assumes, so if everyone else is doing this,

439
00:17:48,600 --> 00:17:50,200
why would I go along with this?

440
00:17:50,200 --> 00:17:52,540
And you remember the whole idea of the Nash equilibrium

441
00:17:52,540 --> 00:17:54,520
is one where each person simultaneously

442
00:17:54,520 --> 00:17:57,640
says, if everyone else is going to do what I will too,

443
00:17:57,640 --> 00:18:00,280
then that can happen.

444
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:01,800
Doesn't have to happen.

445
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:05,240
Remember the example of if you expect everyone in this room

446
00:18:05,240 --> 00:18:07,040
will be in the seats they're currently in,

447
00:18:07,040 --> 00:18:08,280
where do you want to sit?

448
00:18:08,280 --> 00:18:10,000
You want to sit in your current seat.

449
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:11,540
Of course, there's other equilibrium

450
00:18:11,540 --> 00:18:13,840
which can happen too, but there is an equilibrium where

451
00:18:13,840 --> 00:18:16,240
everyone is riding the seats that they're currently in.

452
00:18:16,240 --> 00:18:16,740
OK.

453
00:18:16,740 --> 00:18:19,400
So that's like, I guess, I guess I

454
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:21,800
didn't realize that's a Nash equilibrium because you're

455
00:18:21,800 --> 00:18:25,720
basically saying it's a given that the second part

456
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:27,880
of the agreement, they're going to do that.

457
00:18:27,880 --> 00:18:28,600
So you can't.

458
00:18:28,600 --> 00:18:30,280
Oh, well, similarly, like I said,

459
00:18:30,280 --> 00:18:31,240
it doesn't have to be an agreement.

460
00:18:31,240 --> 00:18:32,560
It can just be an understanding.

461
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:35,800
Not necessarily an agreement, but it's like,

462
00:18:35,800 --> 00:18:36,880
they're going to do that.

463
00:18:36,880 --> 00:18:38,400
Because all I'm saying is this is an equilibrium.

464
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:40,240
So what does it mean to be Nash equilibrium?

465
00:18:40,240 --> 00:18:44,480
It just means that if everyone else thinks it's going to happen,

466
00:18:44,480 --> 00:18:46,880
that if each person thinks that everyone else will do it

467
00:18:46,880 --> 00:18:48,360
and you want to do it too, and it's

468
00:18:48,360 --> 00:18:50,760
true for every single person, then

469
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:53,480
it gets put into the stack of Nash equilibrium.

470
00:18:53,480 --> 00:18:54,360
Right, OK.

471
00:18:54,360 --> 00:18:56,560
Right, the Nash equilibrium stack.

472
00:18:56,560 --> 00:18:57,240
All right.

473
00:18:57,240 --> 00:19:00,560
So again, so what I'm doing, I realize

474
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:02,680
it seems like sleight of hand or trickery,

475
00:19:02,680 --> 00:19:07,160
but it is a strictly clean application of the concept

476
00:19:07,160 --> 00:19:10,360
of Nash equilibrium.

477
00:19:10,360 --> 00:19:12,520
And of course, by the way, if you were doing your thing

478
00:19:12,520 --> 00:19:13,680
where you could say, well, if I did that,

479
00:19:13,680 --> 00:19:14,840
he would switch too, you might, you

480
00:19:14,840 --> 00:19:16,200
could also say you get cooperation

481
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:17,760
in the prisoner's level, right?

482
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:19,680
You could also say, wait a second,

483
00:19:19,680 --> 00:19:22,520
like if I betray him, he'll just betray me,

484
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:24,600
so I better not do it.

485
00:19:24,600 --> 00:19:27,400
So again, so if you go down that route,

486
00:19:27,400 --> 00:19:30,120
then you then actually even think,

487
00:19:30,120 --> 00:19:33,320
even in cases where it seems like there's a clear answer,

488
00:19:33,320 --> 00:19:37,480
you might say, it's not a clear answer after all.

489
00:19:37,480 --> 00:19:38,840
All right.

490
00:19:38,840 --> 00:19:42,240
OK, now, so that's what happens when we do prisoner's lemma

491
00:19:42,240 --> 00:19:43,840
followed by coordination game.

492
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:45,340
What happens if we reverse the order

493
00:19:45,340 --> 00:19:48,320
and we do coordination game followed by prisoner's lemma?

494
00:19:48,320 --> 00:19:51,680
Can we still get more equilibrium then?

495
00:19:51,680 --> 00:19:54,080
Well, how many equilibrium do we have here?

496
00:19:54,080 --> 00:19:54,800
One.

497
00:19:54,800 --> 00:19:56,840
So is there anything you can threaten someone

498
00:19:56,840 --> 00:19:59,240
with if they don't do what you want in the coordination

499
00:19:59,240 --> 00:20:20,800
phase now?


